WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.250
&gt;&gt;Kate Crawford: There is no quick
technical fix to bias.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.870
It’s really tempting to want to think that
there’s going to be some type of silver-bullet

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:12.900
solution that we can just tweak our algorithms
or, you know, use different sorts of training

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.470
data sets, or try to boost
signal in particular ways.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:21.820
The problem of this is that it really doesn’t
look to the deep social and historical issues

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.930
that human data is made from.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:24.930
(music)

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.920
Host: You’re listening to the Microsoft
Research podcast.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.000
A show that brings you closer to the cutting-edge
of technology research and the scientists

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.739
behind it.

00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:40.469
I’m your host Gretchen Huizinga.

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:44.609
Artificial intelligence has captured our imagination
and made many things we would have thought

00:00:44.609 --> 00:00:48.079
impossible only a few years ago
seem commonplace today.

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.559
But AI has also raised some challenging
issues for society writ large.

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:58.589
Enter Dr. Kate Crawford, a principal researcher
at the New York City lab of Microsoft Research.

00:00:58.589 --> 00:01:04.620
Dr. Crawford, along with an illustrious group
of colleagues in computer science, engineering,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:10.140
social science, business and law, has dedicated
her research to addressing the social implications

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:16.200
of AI, including big topics like bias, labor
and automation, rights and liberties

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.320
and ethics and governance.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.280
Today, Dr. Crawford talks about both the promises
and the problems of AI, why when it comes

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:30.250
to data, bigger isn’t necessarily better,
and how even in an era of increasingly complex

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:35.770
technological advances, we can adopt AI design
principles that empower people to shape their

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:41.180
technical tools in ways they would
like to use them most.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:47.090
That and much more on this episode of the
Microsoft Research podcast.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:52.180
(music)

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.850
Host: Welcome Kate Crawford to the podcast.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.580
Great to have you here with us
from New York City.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.120
Kate Crawford: Thank you so much Gretchen.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.620
It’s a pleasure to be here.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:05.420
Host: So, you’re in the, as we’ve said,
New York City lab of Microsoft Research.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.590
What goes on in the Big Apple?

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:07.680
Kate Crawford: Ahhh!

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:08.680
So much.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.610
It’s a city that doesn’t sleep when research
is concerned.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.469
Look, there’s so much going on here.

00:02:13.469 --> 00:02:18.040
It is actually — we a have an abundance
of exciting research initiatives.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.409
Obviously here, we have the Microsoft Research
New York office.

00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:25.549
In here, we have really sort of four groups
writ large.

00:02:25.549 --> 00:02:28.700
We have a really fantastic machine learning
group.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.900
We have a computational social science group.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.040
We have an algorithmic economics group.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.989
And we have the FATE group, which is a group
that I co-founded with my colleagues Hannah

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:44.340
Wallach and Fernando Diaz, and it stands for
fairness, accountability, transparency and ethics.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.999
And that group is coming into
its third year now.

00:02:46.999 --> 00:02:52.220
We really, early on, saw that there were going
to be some real concerns around insuring that

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:58.349
large-scale, decision-making systems were
producing fair results and fair predictions,

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:03.159
and we also needed to start thinking much
more about accountability for decision-making;

00:03:03.159 --> 00:03:05.939
particularly in relation to machine learning
and artificial intelligence.

00:03:05.939 --> 00:03:11.680
And, of course, ethics, which is a very broad
term that can mean all sorts of things.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.959
For us, it means really looking at how people
are affected by a whole range of technologies

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:22.469
that are now touching our lives, be that in
criminal justice or education or in healthcare.

00:03:22.469 --> 00:03:26.540
So that was the reason we formed the FATE
group here in New York.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.829
In addition to what’s happening here at
MSR NYC, there are also other groups.

00:03:31.829 --> 00:03:36.900
There’s Data and Society which is headed by
danah boyd, just around the corner from this building.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:43.280
And then at NYU, we have a brand-new research
institute that I co-founded with Meredith

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.719
Whitaker, called the AI Now Institute.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:51.620
And that’s the first university institute to focus
on the social implications of artificial intelligence.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.879
So, there’s a lot going on in
New York right now.

00:03:53.879 --> 00:03:55.090
Host: I’ll say.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:58.749
You know, I want to ask you some specific
questions about a couple of those things that

00:03:58.749 --> 00:04:03.510
you mentioned, but drill in a little bit on
what computational social science means.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:04.510
Kate Crawford: Yeah.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:10.500
That group is headed by Duncan Watts and realistically,
they are looking at large-scale data to try

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.469
and make sense of particular types of patterns.

00:04:14.469 --> 00:04:20.560
So, what can you learn about how a city is working
when you look at traffic flows, for example.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:26.480
What are the ways in which you could contextualize
people’s search information in order to

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.860
give them different types of
data that could help.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.870
I mean there are lots of things that the CSS
group does here in New York.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:36.320
Host: Well let’s go back to FATE, because
that’s kind of a big interest of yours right now.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.420
Fairness, accountability, 
transparency and ethics.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:39.420
Kate Crawford: Yeah.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.420
Host: And you sort of gave us an overview of why
you started the group and it’s fairly nascent.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.020
Is it having an impact already?

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.580
Is it having any impact that you hoped for
in the high-tech industry?

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:50.580
Kate Crawford: Absolutely.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:51.670
We’ve been really thrilled.

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:56.500
I mean, even though it’s only 3 years old,
in some ways, that’s actually quite established

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.070
for thinking about these issues.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:03.630
I’ve been as a researcher focusing on questions
around fairness and due-process in sort of

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.070
large-scale data systems and machine learning
for over ten years.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:11.740
But it’s really only been in the last 18
months or so that we’ve seen this huge uptick

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:15.110
in interest across both academia and industry.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:20.390
So, we’re starting to see algorithmic accountability
groups emerge in kind of the key technology

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:22.030
companies here in the US.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:24.630
We also have conferences like the FAT/ML conference.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:28.420
Which stands for fairness, accountability
and transparency, you guessed it.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.600
Which has now is now become a blockbuster hit.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:34.720
It actually taking place here in New York
City in just two weeks to a full house and

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.110
an extensive waiting list.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.490
So, it’s really actually taking off.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.730
But we are also seeing it really start to
have impact within Microsoft itself.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:47.600
I mean, here, Hannah and myself for example,
and various product groups who are coming

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.490
up with questions where they think a system
might produce say a discriminatory output,

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:52.490
what should they do?

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:56.420
Or they might have concerns with the data
that a system has been trained on.

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.670
What sorts of questions might you want to ask?

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:03.680
Including all the way through to, what are the
big policy questions that we need to ask here.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:07.640
And we’re doing things like speaking to
the European Commission, to the UN, etc.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:11.480
So, um, for a small group, it’s just four
of us, I think it’s already having a pretty

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:12.990
outsized impact.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:17.190
Host: Interesting that you say you’ve been
doing this for about ten years, which I think

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:22.570
is surprising to a lot of people, with machine
learning coming to the forefront now.

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:26.830
Why, do you think, only in the last 18 months
have we seen an uptick?

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.320
Kate Crawford: I think it has a lot to do
with scale.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:32.940
I mean what’s particularly interesting to
me is that just in the last actually about

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.970
three months, we’ve seen leaders from major
technology companies including Satya Nadella,

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:44.040
including one of the co-founders of Deep Mind,
Mustafa Suleyman, and the head of Google AI,

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.970
all say that fairness and bias were the key
challenges for the next five years.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:54.700
So, it’s gone from being something that
was a relatively bespoke concern, shall we say,

00:06:54.700 --> 00:07:00.780
to becoming front-of-mind for all of the sort
of leaders of the major technology companies.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:03.820
And I think the reason why, and certainly
the reason that Mustafa gave when he was sort

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:08.440
of making his statement about this, is because
if you have a system that’s producing a

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:15.330
discriminatory result say, for example, in
a search, or in ads, that is affecting a billion

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.550
to two billion users a day, easily.

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:22.960
So, at that scale, that can be extremely costly
and extremely dangerous for the people who

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:24.480
are subject to those decisions.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.830
So, I think, in that sense, it’s really
a question of how many people can be affected

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:34.040
by these sorts of systems,
given their truly vast reach.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:40.640
(music)

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:47.060
Host: You recently gave a talk at the NIPS conference
— Neural Information Processing Systems.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.580
And the topic was called
The Trouble with Bias.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.930
What is the trouble with bias, Kate?

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:56.170
Kate Crawford: Well, NIPS is an amazing conference,
I should say, out of the gate.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:57.860
It was a real honor to speak there.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.280
It is, in fact, the largest machine learning
conference in the world.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:05.590
I was speaking in this room as the sort of
opening keynote to around 8,000 people, so

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:10.870
it kind of felt like being at a Van Halen
concert or something extremely stadium rock.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.370
It was extraordinary to see.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:16.560
But I was really interested to talk about
what’s been happening with this concept

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:17.560
of bias.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:22.010
And I looked at, particularly, some of the
cases that we’ve seen emerge that have been

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:23.230
interesting to researchers.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:29.030
So, for example, everything from, if you do
a search in Bing or in Google, for “CEO”

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:34.080
and you do an image search, you’ll find
a lot of images of white men in suits.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.360
And, depending on which way the algorithm
is blowing that day, the first woman that

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.729
you’ll see is, quite often, CEO Barbie.

00:08:42.729 --> 00:08:47.420
So, it raises very troubling questions about
how we represent a gender.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:51.020
And then of course, there are a whole series
of studies coming out now, looking at how

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:52.680
we represent race.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.920
And here we can look at anything from the
way that a training data set like Labeled

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:03.380
Faces in the Wild is around 79% male and 84%
white, so they’re the sorts of populations

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:07.030
that systems that are trained on Labeled Faces
in the Wild will perform best for.

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:09.230
So, that’s in the space of facial recognition.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:13.670
But what I was talking about at NIPS was,
really sharing brand new research that I’ve

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:19.840
been working on with Solon Barocas at Cornell
and Hannah Wallach here at Microsoft Research

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:24.960
and Aaron Shapiro at U Penn, where we’re
looking at the way that bias in computer science

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.361
has traditionally been looked at.

00:09:26.361 --> 00:09:30.880
And basically, the way that it’s been studied
so far, we did a big analysis of all of the

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:36.500
papers, is that it really looks at the types
of harms that cause a type of economic,

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.280
or what we call allocative, harm.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:43.210
So, it means that a system is determined to
be biased if you don’t get a job or if it

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:46.380
decides that you don’t get bail or if you
can’t get access to credit.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:50.230
But there’s a whole range of other harms
which we call representational harms, which

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:53.400
doesn’t mean that you don’t necessarily
get a job, but it might mean that it’s just

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.080
a denigration of a particular category or community.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:01.920
Host: Let’s talk for a minute about the
data sets that actually train our AI models.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.570
As far as the data goes, we’re often told
that bigger is better.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:08.480
But what you just said, suggests this might
not be the case.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.850
Can you explain how bigger data isn’t necessarily
better data?

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:17.290
Kate Crawford: Yeah, I mean there has been
I think this perception, for some time now,

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:20.400
that the more data we have,
the more representative it is.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.500
But that’s simply not the case.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:26.630
If you are over-sampling from a particular
population, we could think about here, you

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:28.060
know, say, for example, Twitter data.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:32.180
There was a period, so five years ago, when
people really thought Twitter data was going

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:37.800
to be the best way to understand what was happening
during a natural disaster or humanitarian crisis.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.140
But it was very, very clear, from early on,
and certainly in some work that I was doing

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:46.580
many years ago, that showed just how skewed
the demographics are of people who were using

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:50.940
Twitter at that time, let alone people who
have access to smartphones.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:55.400
So, depending where you in the world, that
means it’s not particularly a very reliable signal.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:59.660
So, even if you have hundreds of thousands
of data points, if all of those data points

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:05.290
are being drawn from affluent populations,
who live in urban centers, then you’re only

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:08.860
seeing one part of the picture and it’s
going to be extremely difficult for you to

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:10.520
extrapolate from that.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:14.660
And there are very, sort of, related problems
happening with training data right now.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:19.830
Training data is you know, often comes from
sets that have hundreds of thousands, if not

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:23.340
millions, of particular items within them.

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:29.020
But if they are very particularly sampled
from an already skewed pool, then that’s

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:30.080
going to still produce results.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.840
I mean, there have been some really, you know,
interesting examples I think that we can look

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.740
at here. And they come from
all sorts of interesting places.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:40.650
In the case of you know criminal justice,
there’s been a lot of controversy around

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:45.900
the use of the Compass Risk Assessment System,
which essentially tries to predict a risk

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:49.880
score for whether or not somebody will re-offend
as a violent criminal.

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:54.450
But of course, it’s, you know, trained on
data that is historical crime data.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:58.580
And again, many criminologists and social
scientists point to the fact that there is

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:00.940
a long history of racist policing in the US.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:06.240
So, if you’re already coming from a baseline
where people of color and low-income communities

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:11.730
are far more likely to be stopped by the police,
to be arrested by the police, and be generally

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:14.760
surveilled, they will be over-represented
in those samples.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:19.960
And then if you’re training a system on that
data, how do you actually accommodate that?

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:23.280
These are really hard questions and I think
what I’ve certainly learned from looking

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:28.260
at these questions as a researcher is that
there is no quick technical fix to bias.

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:33.100
It’s really tempting to want to think that
there’s going to be some type of silver-bullet

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:38.630
solution, that we can just tweak our algorithms
or use different sorts of training data sets

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:41.700
or try to boost signal in particular ways.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:47.050
The problem of this is that it really doesn’t
look to the deep social and historical issues

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:49.460
that human data is made from.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:51.870
That essentially, data reflects our human
history.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:56.190
And our human history itself has many, many
instances of bias.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:59.940
So, I think what’s so interesting, and when
I talk about the trouble with bias, is that

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:03.940
I think, particularly in computer science,
we tend to scope the problem too narrowly

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:06.440
and to think of it purely as something we
can address technically.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:11.529
I think it’s incredibly important that we
understand it as a socio-technical problem.

00:13:11.529 --> 00:13:16.320
That means addressing these sorts of issues
very much in an interdisciplinary context.

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:20.899
So, if you’re designing a system that is
doing anything to do with criminal justice

00:13:20.899 --> 00:13:24.830
system, you should be working side-by-side
with people who have been doing the most important

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:26.160
work in those fields.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:30.040
This pertains to every one of the domains
of healthcare, education, criminal justice,

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:31.420
policing, you name it.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:35.589
We have area experts who I think have just
been, to this point, kind of left out of those

00:13:35.589 --> 00:13:37.700
development cycles, and we really need them
in the room.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:42.620
Host: Well, I think there’s been a misconception,
first of all that data doesn’t lie.

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:47.600
And it can if you have you know, specific
populations that you’re only representing.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:53.520
But also, this idea of the sort of “separation
of church and state” between technology

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:57.339
and the humanities,
or technology and social science.

00:13:57.339 --> 00:14:02.370
And so, what I’m hearing, not just from
you but over and over, is we have to start

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:07.010
talking, cross-pollinating, silo-busting,
whatever you want to call it, to solve some

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:08.650
of these bigger problems, yeah?

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:09.650
Kate Crawford: Absolutely.

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:10.650
I couldn’t agree more.

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:15.360
I mean this was really one of the really big
motivations behind establishing the AI Now

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:20.290
Institute at NYU, is that we realize we really
needed to create a university center that

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:26.190
was inviting people from all disciplines to
come and collaborate on these sorts of questions.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:29.600
And particularly in terms of issues around,
you know, bias and fairness.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:34.100
But even more broadly, in terms of things
like labor and automation and the future of work,

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:38.360
right through to what happens when we
start applying machine learning to critical

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:40.620
infrastructure, like the
power grid or to hospitals.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:45.640
In order to answer any of those questions,
you kind of need a really deep bench of people

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:50.420
from very different disciplines and we ended
up trying to address that by working with

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:53.600
six different faculty incentives
to establish AI Now.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:58.459
So, it’s a co-production if you will between
computer science, engineering, social science,

00:14:58.459 --> 00:15:02.100
business and law, as well as the Center for
Data Science, really because I think you need

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:05.380
all those people to really make it work.

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:12.000
(music)

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.880
Host: Let’s switch to another topic for
a bit.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:18.740
And that is this concept of autonomous experimentation.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:23.360
With the proliferation of sensors and massive
amounts of data gathering, people may not

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:28.510
be aware much of the time that they are in
fact data and not necessarily with their knowledge

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.260
or consent. Can you speak to that?

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:31.500
Kate Crawford: Oh, absolutely.

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:36.810
I mean I should say the autonomous experimentation
research that we’ve been doing here is very

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:41.700
much a collaborative project which has been
work I’ve been doing alongside people like

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:45.110
Hannah Wallach and Fernando Diaz and Sarah
Bird.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:51.210
And it’s been really fascinating to, essentially
look at how a series of new systems that are

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:56.640
really being deployed far more widely than
people realize, are actually doing forms of

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:59.860
automated experimentation in real-time.

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:03.340
And we were looking at a range of systems
including, say, what happens when you use

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:07.080
a traffic app. You know, just using a mapping 
app that’s saying where is the traffic bad?

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:08.470
Where is it good?

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:12.490
The way these systems are working is that
they’re essentially constantly running experiments,

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:17.140
large-scale experiments, often on hundreds
of thousands of people, simultaneously.

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:18.480
And this can be good.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:21.980
In many cases, it’s for things like you
know, load balancing where people go.

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:26.570
If we all got the same directions to go to,
you know, say, downtown Manhattan from uptown

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:28.140
Manhattan, then the roads would be unusable.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:29.700
They would be completely congested.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:32.910
So, you kind of have to load-balance
between different parts.

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:37.370
But what that also means is that some people
will always be allocated to the less ideal

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:42.080
condition set of that experiment and some
of that will be allocated to the ideal condition set.

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:45.660
Which means that you know, you might be getting
the fastest way to get to work that day, and

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:47.900
somebody else will be getting
a slightly slower way.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:51.380
And now, this sounds absolutely fine when
you’re thinking about you know, perhaps

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:55.500
if you’re just going to work in a few minutes,
either side isn’t going to ruin your day.

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:57.070
But what if you’re going to hospital?

00:16:57.070 --> 00:17:00.600
What if you have a sick kid in the back of
your car and it’s really urgent that you

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:01.820
get to a hospital.

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:05.700
How can you say, “No, I really don’t want
to be allocated into the less ideal group”?

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.720
Or this could happen as well in health apps.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:11.741
You know, how can you indicate that, in experimentation,
which has been used to try and, say, make

00:17:11.741 --> 00:17:16.860
you jog more or do more exercise, that you’re
somebody who’s recovering from injury or

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:19.360
somebody who has a heart condition.

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:23.500
These are the sorts of issues that really
indicated to us that it’s important that

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:25.970
we start doing more work on feedback mechanisms.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:30.299
On what are the sorts of consent mechanisms
that we can think about when people are being

00:17:30.299 --> 00:17:33.559
allocated into experiments, often without
their knowledge.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:35.120
This is very common now.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:40.200
We’re kind of getting used to the fact that AB
experiments at-scale really began with search.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:45.300
But now they’re really moving and submerging
into much more intimate systems that guide

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:50.740
our everyday lives from anything that you’re
using on your phone about your health or your

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:54.690
engagements with friends, right through to
you know, how you spend your time.

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:59.660
So, how do we start to think about the consent
mechanisms around experimentation?

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:02.920
That was the real motivation behind those
series of ongoing studies.

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:09.480
Host: Well and it does speak to this dichotomy
between self-governance and government regulation.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:14.520
And because we are in kind of a Wild West
phase of AI, a lot of things haven’t caught up yet.

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:20.960
However, the European Union has the GDPR that
does attempt to address some of these issues.

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:26.320
What is your thinking on whether we go with
our own oversight, “who is watching the

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:30.170
watch dog” kind of thing or invite regulations.

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:32.560
What’s going on in your mind about that?

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:34.320
Kate Crawford: It’s such a good question.

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:39.070
It’s an incredibly complex question and
unfortunately there are no easy answers here.

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:43.740
I mean, certainly GDPR comes into effect in
May this year and I think it’s going to

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:50.170
be extraordinarily interesting to see what
happens as it begins to be rolled out.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:54.460
Certainly, a lot of the technology companies
have been doing a lot of work to think about

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:57.800
how their products are going to change in
terms of what they’re offering.

00:18:57.800 --> 00:19:01.280
But it will also be interesting to see if
it has flow on effects to other parts of the

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:05.300
world like the US and a whole range of other
countries that aren’t covered by GDPR.

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:10.550
Host: Well, the interesting thing for me is
that say, the European Union has it.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:12.210
It has far-reaching tentacles, right?

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:13.970
It’s not just for Europe.

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:16.580
It’s for anyone who does business with Europe.

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:19.320
And it does as you say represent
a very complex question.

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:20.460
Kate Crawford: It does.

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:25.110
I mean I think about this a lot in terms of
artificial intelligence writ large, and that

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:26.110
term can mean many things.

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:30.010
So, I’m using it here to really refer to
not just sort of machine learning based systems,

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:33.450
but a whole range of other technologies that
fit under the AI banner.

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:37.660
And this is something that is going to have
enormous impacts over the next ten years.

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:42.910
And there’s a lot of attention being paid
to, what are the types of regulatory infrastructures,

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:47.230
what are the types of state and corporate
pressures on these sectors and how is it going

00:19:47.230 --> 00:19:51.300
to change the way that people are
judged by these systems?

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:53.780
As we know China has a social credit score.

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:57.040
Some people find this quite a disturbing system,
but there are many things in the US

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:58.320
that are not dissimilar.

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:03.610
So, we’re already moving quite rapidly into
a state where these systems are being used

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:06.280
to have direct impacts on people’s lives.

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:09.070
And I think there’s going to be a lot of
questions that we have to ask about how to

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:12.680
ensure that that is both ethical and,
I think, equitable.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:13.770
Host: Right.

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:18.940
And that is where, interestingly I think,
some good research could be happening,

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:23.820
both on the technical side and the
social science side, as we address these.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:30.380
And all of the sort of expertise and disciplines
that you talked that are working in the FATE group.

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:35.580
(music)

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:40.020
Host: So, let’s talk for a second about…
there’s so many questions I want to ask you,

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:43.700
Kate, and this is just not really enough
time. So, I’m coming to New York.

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:46.740
I’m going to get into the bad traffic there
and come see you.

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:48.240
Kate Crawford: Great. Wonderful.

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:54.960
Host: Listen, the overarching question for
me right now is about, how do we take these

00:20:54.960 --> 00:21:02.550
big, thorny, prickly, questions… issues,
and start to address them practically?

00:21:02.550 --> 00:21:08.140
What are your thoughts on how we can maybe
re-assert or re-capture our “agency” in

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:09.540
what I’d call a get app world?

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:12.240
Kate Crawford: Yeah, I think that’s a really
fascinating question.

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:16.660
I mean what’s interesting, of course, is
how many systems that touch our lives these

00:21:16.660 --> 00:21:20.620
days that we don’t even have 
a screen interface for. So many,

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:24.780
many major cities, you’re walking down the 
street, you know, your face is being recorded.

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:28.680
Your emotions are being predicted based on
your expressions that day.

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:32.300
You know, your geolocation is being tracked
through your phone.

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:36.410
These are all things that don’t involve
any type of permission structure, a screen,

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:40.679
or even you being aware that, you know, your
data and your movements are being ingested

00:21:40.679 --> 00:21:42.140
by a particular system.

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:47.800
So, I think my concern is that while more
granular permission-based structures are possible,

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:53.590
urban computing has shifted away from the
devices directly in front of us to be embedded

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:57.980
throughout architectures, throughout streets,
throughout so many systems, that are in many

00:21:57.980 --> 00:21:59.630
ways invisible to us.

00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:02.850
And they’re already having a real impact
on how people are being assessed, and the

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:06.880
sort of impacts that they might experience
just walking around the city in a day.

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:12.010
So, I think we are coming up with things that
would’ve been great to have, and would still

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:15.960
be useful in some context, but they don’t
resolve, I think, these much bigger questions

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:20.870
around, what is agency, when you’re really
just a point amongst many, many other data

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:24.960
points and being used by a whole range
of systems that are in many ways

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.880
completely opaque to you?
I think we need to do a lot more work.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:29.140
And certainly, I would agree with you.

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:31.670
I mean, this is an urgent area for research.

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:35.000
We just desperately need more people working
in these areas, both technically and I think

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:37.000
on these more social science perspectives.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:41.730
Host: You know, it’s funny as you’re speaking,
Kate, you actually just identified basically

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:44.190
a generation skip, as it were.

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:49.350
You know like if a country doesn’t have
landline phone infrastructure and goes straight

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:51.330
to cell phones, right?

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:57.160
And so just when we’re thinking we ought
to be more cognizant about giving consent

00:22:57.160 --> 00:23:01.980
to apps and technologies, you’re bringing
up the excellent point that there’s so much

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:05.260
going on that we don’t even
get asked for consent.

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:07.460
Kate Crawford: Absolutely. And also,
I mean, there’s another thing here.

00:23:07.460 --> 00:23:12.980
I think that we’ve really moved beyond the
discussion of, you know, the idea of a single

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:16.470
person, you know, looking at an app and deciding
whether or not you should allow it to have

00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:18.169
access to all of your contacts.

00:23:18.169 --> 00:23:22.570
We’re in a very different state where you
could be using an app or system and you’re

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:25.290
thinking that it’s for one thing but it’s
actually doing something else.

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:29.750
I mean, the classic case here is say, you
know, sort of, the Pokémon Go craze where

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:34.780
you are out to sort of catch little Pokémons in a
sort of augmented reality environment in cities.

00:23:34.780 --> 00:23:40.960
But that became… or was being used to harvest
a massive training data set to really generate

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:43.660
sort of new maps and geo-locative data.

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:47.510
So, people, in some ways, think that they’re
doing one thing, but their data is being used

00:23:47.510 --> 00:23:52.799
to train a completely different type of system
that, you know, they may have no idea about.

00:23:52.799 --> 00:23:56.910
So, I think, again, this idea that we’re
even aware of what we’re participating in,

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:58.490
I think, has really moved on.

00:23:58.490 --> 00:24:03.220
Host: Yeah, again with the idea that we’re
on a podcast and no one can see me shaking

00:24:03.220 --> 00:24:11.559
my head. This does bring up questions about the practical potential solutions that we have.

00:24:11.559 --> 00:24:18.510
Is there any, from your end, any recommendation,
not just about what the problems are, but

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:21.820
who should be tackling them and how, I mean…

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:22.940
Kate Crawford: Yeah, absolutely.

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:27.920
I’m actually, I mean, for a while, while
our conversation does cover some really thorny

00:24:27.929 --> 00:24:32.150
and I think, you know, quite confronting questions
that we’re going to need to contend with

00:24:32.150 --> 00:24:37.121
as researchers and as an industry, I think
that there’s also a lot of hope and there’s

00:24:37.121 --> 00:24:38.830
a lot that we can do.

00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:43.360
One of the things that I work on at the AI
Now institute is we release an annual State

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:47.540
of AI report. And in that report, we make
a series of recommendations every year.

00:24:47.549 --> 00:24:51.169
And in the last report, which just came out
a couple of months ago, we really sort of

00:24:51.169 --> 00:24:55.880
made some very clear and direct recommendations
about how to address some of these core problems.

00:24:55.880 --> 00:25:01.220
One of them is we just think that before releasing
an AI system, particularly in a high-stakes

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:05.400
domain, so, all of those things we’ve chatted
about like, you know, healthcare, criminal

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:09.920
justice, education, that companies should
be running rigorous pre-release trials to

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:14.010
ensure that those systems aren’t going to
amplify, you know, errors and biases.

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:16.440
And I see that as a fairly basic request.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:19.920
I mean it’s certainly something we expect
of pharmaceutical drugs before they go on

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:22.400
the market. That they’ve been tested,
that they won’t cause harm.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:24.480
The same is true of, you know, consumer devices.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:27.370
Ideally, they don’t blow up, you know,
with some exceptions.

00:25:27.370 --> 00:25:31.830
You really want to make sure that there’s
some pretty extensive testing and trials.

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:33.610
And then trials that can be sort of publicly-shared.

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:37.539
That we can say that we have assessed that
this system, you know, doesn’t produce disparate

00:25:37.539 --> 00:25:39.980
impact on different types of communities.

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.400
I think it’s also really important that
another recommendation, another thing we can

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:47.590
do is, you know, after releasing these sorts
of complex, often, you know, algorithmically-driven

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:52.570
systems, um, should I say, that we just continue
to monitor their use across different contexts

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:53.560
and communities.

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:57.820
Often, a type of system is released, and it’s
assumed that it’s just going to work for

00:25:57.820 --> 00:26:00.610
everybody for an indefinite period of time.

00:26:00.610 --> 00:26:01.870
It’s just not the case.

00:26:01.870 --> 00:26:07.549
I was looking at a study recently that suggested
that medical health data has a 4-month half-life.

00:26:07.549 --> 00:26:12.491
That’s 4 months before it becomes out-of-date,
before the data in that training data set

00:26:12.491 --> 00:26:16.730
is actually going to become contradicted by
things that have happened after the fact.

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:19.919
So, we need to keep thinking about,
how long is a system relevant?

00:26:19.919 --> 00:26:22.760
How might it be performing differently for
different communities?

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:26.060
So, this is another recommendation that we
feel very strongly about.

00:26:26.060 --> 00:26:29.520
But there are many more, and if people are
interested in particular types of research

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:35.010
questions or concrete steps that we can take
moving forward, the AI Now 2017 report has

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:36.850
a lot of those in there for more reading.

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:37.850
Host: Yeah.

00:26:37.850 --> 00:26:42.510
I would actually encourage our listeners to
get it, and read it, because it is fascinating,

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:47.640
and it addresses things from a, sort of, an
upstream point-of-view, which is I think where

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:50.500
we need to go.
We’re a bit downstream.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:54.760
Because we’ve released the Kraken,
in many ways, of AI.

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:59.460
It’s like suddenly they’re… in fact,
back to the original… you know the last

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:03.960
18 months, I think part of the reason, maybe,
that we’re seeing an increased interest

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:08.800
is because people are realizing, “Hey this
is happening, and it’s happening to me,

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:10.060
and now I care!”

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:13.460
Prior to that, I just kind of went along and
didn’t pay attention.

00:27:13.460 --> 00:27:15.360
So, attention is a good thing.

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:16.500
Let me ask you this.

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:20.720
You mentioned a concept from architecture
called “desire lines” in a talk that I

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:25.080
heard, and I loved it in the sense that it’s
like shortcuts in public spaces, where, you

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:29.790
know, you find a path on the grass just because
people don’t want to go around on the concrete.

00:27:29.790 --> 00:27:34.440
And I would say things like Napster and BitTorrent
are sort of examples of technical desire lines,

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:36.410
where it’s like I’m going to go around.

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:40.429
Is there anything in that space that’s sort
of happening now from a grassroots perspective,

00:27:40.429 --> 00:27:44.470
where we’re sort of “take back the night”
kind of thing, in the AI world?

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:45.943
Kate Crawford: Yes, I love this.

00:27:45.943 --> 00:27:47.049
You know, I think there is.

00:27:47.049 --> 00:27:52.360
There are some really nice examples of very
small grassroots efforts that have been extremely

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:58.140
successful in doing things like essentially,
creating spaces of anonymity where you’re

00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:04.440
much more protected from your data being harvested,
for reasons that you may or may not have agreed to.

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:09.700
Even things like the private messaging service
signal, which again was created really by

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:12.090
you know, one guy and a few of his friends.

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:17.059
Moxie Marlinspike has been I think very much
a champion that individuals can you know,

00:28:17.059 --> 00:28:21.650
create these types of systems that can create
more freedom and more agency for people.

00:28:21.650 --> 00:28:23.500
And there are others, too.

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:28.300
I think it’s going to be interesting to think about 
how that will happen in an AI-driven world.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:31.539
And even, I think, in the major technology
companies, I think it’s really important

00:28:31.539 --> 00:28:35.770
to create ways that people can start to shape
these tools in the way that they’d most

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:40.050
like to use them, and to give some space for
sort of desire lines where you can say, well

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:43.429
I don’t actually want my AI system to work
this way, or I don’t want it to have access

00:28:43.429 --> 00:28:44.450
to this information.

00:28:44.450 --> 00:28:48.890
But how can I train it to do what I want it
to do, to make it an advocate for me.

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:53.010
So, rather than serving the interests of,
you know, just a particular company, it’s

00:28:53.010 --> 00:28:57.280
really there as my agent, as someone who’s
going to look out for things that I care about.

00:28:57.280 --> 00:28:59.250
These are things that we can
think of as design principles.

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:02.750
And certainly, something that people do talk
about a lot at Microsoft Research.

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:08.040
And it’s something that, I think, is really exciting
and inspiring for more work to happen now.

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:09.380
Host: I can’t agree more.

00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:15.520
It feels like there’s a “call” both
to the technical designers and makers,

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:19.700
and the end users that need to say
hey, I need to pay attention.

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:23.040
I just can’t be lazy.
I need to take agency.

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:24.260
Kate Crawford: I think that’s
absolutely right.

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:28.280
Host: Kate, before we go, what thoughts or
advice would you leave with our listeners,

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:33.960
many of whom are aspiring researchers, that
might have an interest in the social impact of AI.

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:38.360
I would say go broad on this for both computer
scientists, social scientists, any kind of,

00:29:38.370 --> 00:29:40.409
you know… the interdisciplinary crowd.

00:29:40.409 --> 00:29:41.500
Kate Crawford: Wow.

00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:44.030
Well, first of all, I’d say, welcome!

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:47.580
You’re actually asking some of the most
important questions that can be asked right

00:29:47.580 --> 00:29:49.700
now in the research world.

00:29:49.700 --> 00:29:55.870
And it would be amazing to see people really
start to dig into specific domain questions,

00:29:55.870 --> 00:29:59.720
specific tools, and really start to say, you
know, what kind of world do we want to be

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:01.930
living in, and how can our
tools best serve us there?

00:30:01.930 --> 00:30:07.570
In terms of resources that you can go to now,
there really are some great conferences.

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:12.000
Even the big you know, machine-learning conferences
like NIPS have workshops focused on things

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:14.210
like fairness and accountability.

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:16.600
We have the FAT/ML conference which is annual.

00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:20.480
But there’s also, you know, the AI Now conferences
which happen every year.

00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:25.789
And a lot of, I think, discussion that’s
been happening in a series of groups and reading

00:30:25.789 --> 00:30:28.650
groups in various cities that people can connect with.

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:32.370
And I just think there’s like a thriving
research community now that I’m certainly

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:36.800
starting to see grow very rapidly because
these questions are so pressing.

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:42.760
So, in essence, I’d say regardless of your
field, AI is going to be changing the way

00:30:42.760 --> 00:30:44.409
we think and work.

00:30:44.409 --> 00:30:47.350
And that means that, likely if you’re a
researcher, this is something that you want

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:48.360
to start caring about.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:52.150
And please, if there are ways that I can help
or if people want to get in touch, they are

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:53.150
welcome to do so.

00:30:53.150 --> 00:30:57.420
Host: Kate Crawford, thank you for taking
time out of your schedule.

00:30:57.420 --> 00:30:59.690
Like I said, I want to keep
talking to you forever.

00:30:59.690 --> 00:31:01.750
Kate Crawford: Such a pleasure.

00:31:01.750 --> 00:31:05.500
Thank you so much, Gretchen.
It was great.

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:10.640
Host: To learn more about Dr. Kate Crawford’s
work on the social impacts of AI, and to download

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:16.240
the most recent report from AI Now, visit
Microsoft.com/research.

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:18.960
(music)

